This is the first major concept you need to understand.

Suppose a company has:

1000 employees
500 computers
50 servers

Without centralized management, you could have

Computer 1 → local users
Computer 2 → local users
Computer 3 → local users
...
Computer 500 → local users

This becomes difficult to manage.

Instead, the company creates a central system:

                Active Directory
                       |
        +--------------+--------------+
        |              |              |
      Users         Computers       Groups
        |              |              |
     Bilal           PC-001         Linux-Team
     Ahmed           PC-002         HR-Team
     John            PC-003         IT-Team

Active Directory allows administrators to centrally manage:

users
computers
groups
passwords
access
policies
authentication
permissions

=> Things you can do in AD

Create employee → Active Directory
Disable employee → Active Directory
Reset password → Active Directory
Add user to group → Active Directory

=> Note:-

Authentication → Who are you?
Authorization  → What are you allowed to do?
Active Directory → Central place to manage identities, users, groups and policies.
